{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the land shall rest a sabbath unto YHWH.</b> The Holy One, blessed be He, sanctified the seventh in days, in years, and in shemittot. He sanctified the seventh in days — this is the Sabbath — and called it sabbath, as it is said (Exodus 20:10): ״But the seventh day is a sabbath unto YHWH your Elohim.״ He sanctified the seventh in years — this is shemittah — and called it too sabbath, as it is said: ״ושבתה הארץ שבת לה'.״ He sanctified the seventh in shemittot — this is the Jubilee — and called it holy, as it is said: ״For it is the Jubilee; it shall be holy unto you.״" "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): ושבתה הארץ שבת לה' — that the land should lie fallow from all human labor in the seventh year, and one should not engage at all in working the soil, neither in plowing nor in sowing, as He said: ״Six years you shall sow your field, etc., and in the seventh year shall be a sabbath of solemn rest for the land.״ And even what grows of itself without plowing is forbidden to harvest — this is ״את ספיח קצירך לא תקצור״: what grows of itself and is attached to the harvest of the previous year is called ספיח (spiah), from the language of (Isaiah 14:1): ״ונספחו על בית יעקב.״ And likewise a vine that was not worked, not hoed, and not pruned is called נזיר (nazir), because they separated it (הזירו) from it as something that is not his. And the general principle of the matter is that one is not permitted to conduct himself in his field and vineyard with the conduct of a master at all, but rather all its fruits are ownerless to all the world, so that even the lowliest of Israel may acquire them equally with him. And therefore the Torah commanded this commandment — that all kinds of dominion and lordship among those below be nullified in the working of the land — so that a person may reflect in his heart that the essence of lordship and dominion belongs only to the Master of all, blessed be He." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ושבתה הארץ שבת לה' — corresponding to 'and one is desolate' (וחד חריב), which is altogether sabbath and rest for the life of the worlds, and it is the World to Come that follows the resurrection. And this is the reason for shemittah — that the land lies fallow and desolate of all work in the seventh year. And because of this the Torah was strict regarding shemittah more than all other negative prohibitions, and decreed exile upon it, as it is written (Leviticus 26:34): ״Then shall the land enjoy her sabbaths,״ and so our Rabbis of blessed memory taught: exile comes to the world on account of perversion of justice and on account of the shemittah of the land. And behold, one who denies the commandment of shemittah is like one who denies the renewal of the world and the World to Come, and this is clear."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The Aramaic phrase 'וחד חריב' ('and one is desolate') is left untranslated as it belongs to Kabbalistic terminology from Ramban's school; per voice rules the Kabbalah stays cryptic."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:18.860562556Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}